  * <p>Nothing tells the server that the launcher has drawn, and nothing has to: an empty display is a
  * single colour, and so is the starting window Android puts up while the launcher starts, everywhere
  * but its centre. The first frame that is not is the desktop.
+ *
+ * <p>What the pixels cannot tell is <em>whose</em> screen this is. A display with no task on it is
+ * whatever the platform puts there, and on several phones that is the phone's own home screen — the
+ * phone's wallpaper, its icons — from the moment the display exists until the computer has finished
+ * installing the launcher and started it, four or five seconds later. The probe saw that as "not a
+ * fill", opened the splash onto it, and the user was shown a second home screen before the real
+ * desktop arrived ("dex home double keno?"). So the probe is not allowed to open anything until the
+ * computer says the launcher has been STARTED: it writes {@link #CAN_OPEN} after its own
+ * {@code am start}, and until then the splash stays over whatever is behind it. The starting window
+ * the launcher puts up is a single opaque colour, so the moment the gate opens is still not read as
+ * the desktop — pixels alone decide that, from there on, exactly as before.
  */
 public final class DesktopSplash {
 
     public static final String FILE = "/data/local/tmp/oadx-desktop-splash";
 
+    /**
+     * The computer has started the launcher on the display: the splash may look at the pixels now.
+     *
+     * <p>Written by the computer right after its {@code am start} (adb.rs, adb_start_launcher) and
+     * removed by {@link #claim()} with the request, so a marker from a previous session can never
+     * open this one's splash. Absent — or the desktop was started by something that does not write
+     * it — the splash simply waits out its own give-up clock.
+     */
+    public static final String CAN_OPEN = "/data/local/tmp/oadx-desktop-launcher-started";
+
+    /** How often the marker is looked for, in ms. Opening is a one-way door; this is not hot. */
+    private static final long CAN_OPEN_EVERY_MS = 200;
+
+    private static boolean canOpen;
+    private static long canOpenAt;
+
     /** Older than this, the file is a desktop that never started, not a request. */
     private static final long FRESH_MS = 60_000;
 
